"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][9]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M9-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M9-L1 – Objective functions, constraints and optimality<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor: Numerical methods and optimisation for learning algorithms (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/compulsory elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L5<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish objective functions,\nfeasible sets and optimality conditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a learning problem to a mathematical task<\/h2>\n<p>A data platform must set two control variables:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor computing power and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor storage capacity, both in normalised capacity units. A synthetic\ncost function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x,y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nevaluates a setting, not the probability that it is correct.\nOptimisation means finding the feasible setting with the smallest value.\nModel training, for example, replaces the cost with an empirical\nprediction loss plus regularisation. The choice of objective function\nremains a modelling decision: minimising computing time may conflict\nwith reliability or energy consumption.<\/p>\n<p>The general notation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\min_{z\\in C} f(z)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe feasible set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis determined by equations\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_j(z)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ninequalities\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_i(z)\\le 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand, where applicable, discrete requirements. A minimum is global if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(z^*)\\le f(z)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nholds for every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z\\in C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\na local minimum need only satisfy the condition in a neighbourhood\nwithin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nExistence is not automatic: on the open interval\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas an infimum of zero but no minimiser.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Geometry of a constrained problem<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001525_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Zulässiges Dreieck x+y≤4 und Niveaulinien um (3; 2); Minimum bei (2,5; 1,5).\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Zulässiges Dreieck x+y≤4 und Niveaulinien\num (3; 2); Minimum bei (2,5; 1,5).<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Constrained minimum.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>In the teaching example, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x,y)=(x-3)^2+(y-2)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsubject to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x,y\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+y\\le4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe level curves are circles centred on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe unconstrained optimum is infeasible. The nearest point of the\ntriangle on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+y=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis obtained by subtracting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.5,0.5)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom the centre. The solution is therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2.5,1.5)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram shows how the smallest attainable level curve touches the\nboundary. A feasible boundary point need not have a vanishing\ngradient.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Stationarity and curvature<\/h2>\n<p>At an interior local minimum of a differentiable function,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla f(z^*)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis necessary. The second derivative helps distinguish cases: for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=x^3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nzero is stationary but not an extremum; for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=-x^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nzero is a maximum. A positive definite Hessian at a stationary point of\na twice continuously differentiable function guarantees a strict local\nminimum. For convex functions, stationarity suffices for global\noptimality. With several local minima, however, a general numerical\nsolver often returns only a candidate.<\/p>\n<p>At the constrained solution,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla f=(-1,-1)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe negative gradient direction would violate the budget. Along the\nboundary line, its scalar product with the feasible tangent vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,-1)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis zero. The boundary condition replaces unconstrained stationarity here\n(see <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L4<\/a>).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Increase the budget to 4.6 units. The centre uses 5 units in total,\nso subtract 0.2 from both coordinates. The solution is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2.8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1.8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2.8,1.8)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith an objective value of 0.08. With a budget of 5 or more, the centre\nitself is feasible. A higher budget does not improve this objective\nfunction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/\">Boyd/Vandenberghe:\nConvex Optimization (2004), Ch. 2–5, 9–11<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.cornell.edu/courses/cs3780/2026sp/lectures/lecturenote07.html\">Cornell\nCS 4/5780: Gradient Descent (and Beyond)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M9-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M9-L2 – Gradient methods and learning rates<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor: Numerical methods and optimisation for learning algorithms (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/compulsory elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L4<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L5<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate gradient steps and\nassess stable learning rates for quadratic problems.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The direction of steepest local descent<\/h2>\n<p>For a differentiable function, the Taylor approximation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(w+d)\\approx f(w)+\\nabla f(w)^Td<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAmong directions with Euclidean norm one, the direction opposite to the\nnormalised gradient minimises this linear expression. This gives the\ngradient descent method:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{k+1}=w_k-\\eta_k\\nabla f(w_k),\\qquad \\eta_k&gt;0.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The gradient determines the direction; the learning rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndetermines the step length relative to its magnitude. Different\nparameter scales change this geometry. A step is a local decision, not a\nguarantee of finding the global minimum.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Learning rate in a verifiable example<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001623_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Vier Iterationsfolgen: monoton, oszillierend konvergent, dauerhaft pendelnd und divergent.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Vier Iterationsfolgen: monoton,\noszillierend konvergent, dauerhaft pendelnd und divergent.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Learning rate and stability.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(w)=2(w-3)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f&#39;(w)=4(w-3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe error\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_k=w_k-3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nobeys\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>η<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_{k+1}=(1-4\\eta)e_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>η<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|1-4\\eta|&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit follows that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;\\eta&lt;0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nStarting at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe first two values are 1.2 and 1.92; with 0.4 they are 4.8 and 1.92.\nBoth methods reduce the magnitude of the error by a factor of 0.6, but\nonly the second alternates sides. At 0.5 the parameter oscillates\nbetween zero and six; above this rate the error grows. The diagram shows\nthe different trajectories, including divergence.<\/p>\n<p>For a multidimensional quadratic problem\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>w<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>w<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(w)=\\frac12(w-w^*)^TH(w-w^*)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith symmetric positive definite\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\neach eigendirection has its own factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\eta\\lambda_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA fixed positive learning rate smaller than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2/\\lambda_{\\max}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nguarantees convergence. If the eigenvalues differ greatly, the slow\ndirection may require many steps.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Implementation and step selection<\/h2>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>w <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> k <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    w <span class=\"op\">-=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> (w <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(w, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span> <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>A backtracking line search initially selects a larger step size and\nreduces it until, for example, the Armijo condition\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>η<\/mi><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(w+\\eta d)\\le f(w)+c\\eta\\nabla f(w)^Td<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;c&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nholds. Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust be a descent direction. For expensive data losses, the additional\nevaluations cost computing time; with changing mini-batches, a direct\ncomparison is noisy. Scaled features, sensible initial values and\nlimited step counts improve practical robustness.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Which constant learning rate reaches the minimum in one step in the\nscalar example? With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=1/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe error factor becomes zero. This value cannot be transferred to an\narbitrary non-quadratic problem. For highly anisotropic functions, a\nsingle learning rate is a compromise.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.cornell.edu/courses/cs3780/2026sp/lectures/lecturenote07.html\">Cornell\nCS 4/5780: Gradient Descent (and Beyond)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://stat243.berkeley.edu/fall-2024/units/unit11-optim.html\">UC\nBerkeley STAT 243: Optimization (2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M9-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M9-L3 – Newton and quasi-Newton methods<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor: Numerical methods and optimisation for learning algorithms (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/compulsory elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L5<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L6<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive Newton and quasi-Newton\nsteps and understand their limitations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Curvature as additional information<\/h2>\n<p>The gradient indicates how steeply the objective function rises at\nthe current point. Newton’s method also uses the local change in that\ngradient. The quadratic approximation is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>d<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><msup><mi>d<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>H<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>∇<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(d)=f(w)+g^Td+\\frac12d^THd,\\quad g=\\nabla f(w),\\quad H=\\nabla^2 f(w).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For positive definite\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis approximation has the unique minimum given by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Hd=-g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSolve the linear system rather than explicitly computing the inverse\nmatrix. The step is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_{k+1}=w_k+\\alpha d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\npure Newton uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNear a regular minimum, Newton’s method can converge quadratically under\nsufficiently smooth curvature. Outside that neighbourhood, safeguards\nare needed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example: adjusting two parameters<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001656_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Elliptische Niveaulinien und Bewegungen vom Start (2; 2) nach (1,8; 0,2) bzw. (0; 0).\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Elliptische Niveaulinien und Bewegungen\nvom Start (2; 2) nach (1,8; 0,2) bzw. (0; 0).<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Gradient step and Newton step.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(u,v)=\\frac12(u^2+9v^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand start at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe gradient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>18<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,18)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand Hessian\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{diag}(1,9)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=(-2,-2)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe objective is exactly quadratic; Newton reaches\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nimmediately. A gradient step with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninstead reaches\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1.8,0.2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram shows the markedly different curvatures and the first\nmovement of both methods. For a general function, the Hessian can be\nindefinite or singular. The computed Newton step may then go uphill or\nbe undefined.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>BFGS and limited memory<\/h2>\n<p>Quasi-Newton methods learn curvature from successive gradients. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=w_{k+1}-w_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=g_{k+1}-g_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>s<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=(y^Ts)^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe inverse BFGS update is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_{k+1}=(I-\\rho sy^T)B_k(I-\\rho ys^T)+\\rho ss^T.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napproximates the inverse Hessian. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis positive definite and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y^Ts&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe next approximation remains positive definite; the direction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-B_{k+1}g_{k+1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a descent direction for a non-zero gradient. Suitable line searches,\ndamping or skipped updates help when curvature values are problematic.\nDense BFGS requires memory quadratic in the number of parameters. L-BFGS\nretains only a limited number of vector pairs and is therefore\nattractive for large smooth problems.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.optimize <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> minimize<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>H <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.diag([<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">9.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>f <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> w: <span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> w <span class=\"op\">@<\/span> H <span class=\"op\">@<\/span> w<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>g <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> w: H <span class=\"op\">@<\/span> w<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>w <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(w <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> np.linalg.solve(H, <span class=\"op\">-<\/span>g(w)), <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>res <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> minimize(f, w, jac<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>g, method<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;BFGS&#39;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> res.success <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.linalg.norm(res.x) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1e-5<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=(1,0)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=(2,0)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_0=I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth bracketed factors are diagonal with entries zero and one. The last\nsummand contributes 0.5 to the first diagonal element. Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_1=\\operatorname{diag}(0.5,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe observed curvature of two is correctly inverted along the first\ndirection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.cornell.edu/courses/cs3780/2026sp/lectures/lecturenote07.html\">Cornell\nCS 4/5780: Gradient Descent (and Beyond)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://stat243.berkeley.edu/fall-2024/units/unit11-optim.html\">UC\nBerkeley STAT 243: Optimization (2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.helsinki.fi/u/ahonkela/teaching/compstats1/book/automatic-differentiation-and-optimisation.html\">A.\nHonkela, Computational Statistics I: Automatic differentiation and\noptimisation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M9-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M9-L4 – Convexity, projections and duality<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor: Numerical methods and optimisation for learning algorithms (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/compulsory elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L5<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain convex sets and\nfunctions, projections and primal-dual certificates.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why convexity is useful<\/h2>\n<p>A set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis convex if every line segment connecting two points in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlies entirely within it. A function on a convex set is convex if, for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le t\\le1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe following holds:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(tx+(1-t)y)\\le tf(x)+(1-t)f(y).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A local minimum is then also global: if a point with a lower function\nvalue existed, intermediate points arbitrarily close to the local\nminimum would also have lower values. Strict convexity guarantees\nuniqueness, provided a minimum exists at all. In learning, quadratic\nlosses and many classical regularisers are convex; deep neural networks\nare generally not convex in their joint parameters.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Feasibility after every step<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001670_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Rechteck [0,2]×[0,1], Versuch (2,7; 1,4) und Projektion auf (2; 1).\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Rechteck [0,2]×[0,1], Versuch (2,7; 1,4)\nund Projektion auf (2; 1).<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Projection onto a feasible set.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The Euclidean projection\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_C(v)=\\arg\\min_{z\\in C}\\|z-v\\|_2^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexists and is unique for non-empty, closed, convex\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe projected gradient algorithm sets\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w^+=P_C(w-\\eta\\nabla f(w))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the rectangle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>×<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=[0,2]\\times[0,1]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\neach coordinate is clipped to its interval. Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1.5,0.5)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand an unconstrained estimate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2.7<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1.4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2.7,1.4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyield\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram shows the infeasible trial point and its projection. For a\ngeneral polyhedron, projection is usually itself an optimisation\nproblem; clipping individual coordinates is then insufficient.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Duality: obtaining a bound rather than relying on hope<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\min_x f(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsubject to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g_i(x)\\le0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe Lagrangian\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>g<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal L(x,\\lambda)=f(x)+\\sum_i\\lambda_i g_i(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_i\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndefines the dual function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">inf<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(\\lambda)=\\inf_x\\mathcal L(x,\\lambda)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt every feasible point,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal L\\le f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\ntherefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a lower bound on the primal optimal value. Maximising\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the best such bound. Under suitable regularity conditions (such as\nSlater’s condition for convex problems), strong duality can hold.<\/p>\n<p>In the example\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\min_x(x-3)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsubject to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\le2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^*=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^*=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nminimising\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-3)^2+\\lambda(x-2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=3-\\lambda/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(\\lambda)=\\lambda-\\lambda^2/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe dual maximum occurs at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand has value one. The gap is zero. The KKT conditions are satisfied\nhere: primal and dual feasibility, stationarity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2(x-3)+\\lambda=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand complementary slackness\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda(x-2)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a convex differentiable problem, these conditions are sufficient;\nwithout suitable regularity, not every optimum need have\nmultipliers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>With an upper bound of 4,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis feasible and the constraint is inactive. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe KKT conditions are satisfied and the optimal lower cost bound is\nzero. An inactive constraint needs no positive multiplier.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/\">Boyd/Vandenberghe:\nConvex Optimization (2004), Ch. 2–5, 9–11<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M9-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M9-L5 – Stochastic gradients and mini-batches<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor: Numerical methods and optimisation for learning algorithms (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/compulsory elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L4<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L2<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate mini-batch gradients\nand explain their trade-off between computational cost and variance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>When the full gradient becomes too expensive<\/h2>\n<p>Many learning problems minimise the mean of individual losses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><msubsup><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(w)=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n\\ell_i(w)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe full gradient requires all\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontributions. If instead we draw\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent indices\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nuniformly with replacement, we obtain<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>g<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>b<\/mi><\/mfrac><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>b<\/mi><\/munderover><mi>∇<\/mi><msub><mi>ℓ<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat g_B(w)=\\frac1b\\sum_{j=1}^b\\nabla\\ell_{I_j}(w).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>w<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>g<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E(\\hat g_B)=\\nabla F(w)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe covariance of the mean is then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Sigma(w)/b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the covariance of a single random gradient. Sampling without\nreplacement requires a different finite population correction; unequal\nsampling probabilities require appropriate weights. An update with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><msub><mover><mi>g<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w^+=w-\\eta\\hat g_B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan also increase the total loss.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Calculating a small data model in full<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001517_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Drei Parameterverläufe mit Vollgradient, einem und zwei zufällig gezogenen Messwerten pro Schritt.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Drei Parameterverläufe mit Vollgradient,\neinem und zwei zufällig gezogenen Messwerten pro Schritt.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Stochastic and full gradients.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>We estimate a typical duration from four normalised measurements\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=(1,3,5,7)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell_i(w)=\\frac12(w-a_i)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe minimum of the mean cost occurs at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w^*=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe individual gradients are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,-1,-3,-5)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntheir mean is minus two and their population variance is five. A single\nobservation therefore gives variance 5, whereas an independent batch of\nfour draws gives variance 1.25. A batch containing measurements one and\nseven has gradient minus two. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w^+=2.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>The diagram shows three trajectories calculated from the same\nstarting point: the full gradient, individual random observations and\nmini-batches. With a constant learning rate, noise remains visible\nespecially near the optimum. The particular realisation depends on the\nrandom seed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Computing time, epochs and learning rates<\/h2>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rng <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"dv\">17<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>a <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">5.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">7.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>w <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> k <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">200<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    batch <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> rng.choice(a, size<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, replace<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    w <span class=\"op\">-=<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span> <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> (k <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>)) <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> (w <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> batch).mean()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(w <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>An epoch corresponds to one pass through the training set; when\nsampling with replacement it often means only a nominal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nprocessed examples. Compare methods by the number of processed data\npoints and runtime, not just updates: a batch can be computed in\nparallel and achieves lower variance, but requires more computation.\nDecreasing learning rates can dampen noise over time; for classical\nstochastic approximation, under further assumptions,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_k\\eta_k=\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><msubsup><mi>η<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_k\\eta_k^2&lt;\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare common conditions. Validation data must not be used to form\ngradients.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>What is the variance of the batch gradient for two independent draws?\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5/2=2.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf all four measurements are processed exactly once instead of drawing\nfour randomly, the full gradient is exact and its sampling variance is\nzero.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://stat243.berkeley.edu/fall-2024/units/unit11-optim.html\">UC\nBerkeley STAT 243: Optimization (2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.helsinki.fi/u/ahonkela/teaching/compstats1/book/automatic-differentiation-and-optimisation.html\">A.\nHonkela, Computational Statistics I: Automatic differentiation and\noptimisation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M9-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M9-L6 – Numerical methods for large systems and approximation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor: Numerical methods and optimisation for learning algorithms (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/compulsory elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L4<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L7<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L3<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish conditioning,\niterative linear solvers and low-rank approximation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A large system of equations is not a small matrix<\/h2>\n<p>Linear systems\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Ax=b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noccur, for example, in Newton steps or model fitting. A dense matrix of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndouble-precision values requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><msup><mi>d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8d^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbytes. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100000<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=100000<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis is 80 billion bytes (80 GB decimal), excluding vectors and working\ncopies. If only nearby neighbours are connected, sparse storage can be\nconsiderably smaller. An operator may need only a function that computes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Av<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwithout explicitly constructing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Residual, error and conditioning<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001685_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Residualnorm 0,0001 gegenüber Lösungsfehlernorm 1 auf logarithmischer Achse.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Residualnorm 0,0001 gegenüber\nLösungsfehlernorm 1 auf logarithmischer Achse.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Small residual, large solution error.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For an approximation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tilde x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe residual is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=b-A\\tilde x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe solution error is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x-\\tilde x=A^{-1}r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor an invertible matrix, the condition number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa_2(A)=\\sigma_{\\max}/\\sigma_{\\min}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexplains why a small relative residual does not always guarantee a small\nrelative solution error. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\operatorname{diag}(1,10^{-4})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=(1,10^{-4})^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=(1,1)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tilde x=(1,0)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe residual norm is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{-4}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbut the error norm is one; the condition number is 10,000. The diagram\ncontrasts the two quantities. With full column rank,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^TA<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas the squared 2-norm condition number of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nnormal equations are therefore often unsuitable for ill-conditioned\nleast-squares problems.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Iterative solvers and approximate models<\/h2>\n<p>For symmetric positive definite matrices, the conjugate gradient\nmethod uses search directions orthogonal with respect to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt needs matrix-vector products rather than dense factorisation. In\nexact arithmetic it solves a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-dimensional\nsystem in at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsteps; in practice, a residual tolerance governs termination. A positive\ndefinite preconditioner, for example a suitable diagonal approximation\nto the inverse, can improve convergence. LSQR handles large rectangular\nleast-squares systems using products with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.sparse <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> diags<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.sparse.linalg <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> cg<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>A <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> diags([<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">4.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">8.<\/span>], <span class=\"bu\">format<\/span><span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;csr&#39;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>b <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">8.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">24.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x, status <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> cg(A, b, rtol<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-10<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> status <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.allclose(x, [<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>])<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>For a data matrix with singular values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>⋯<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_1\\ge\\cdots<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na\nrank-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napproximation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>U<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><msubsup><mi>V<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_k=U_k\\Sigma_k V_k^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan save memory. Its Frobenius error is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{\\sum_{i&gt;k}\\sigma_i^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor singular values 9, 4 and 3, the error is five at rank one and three\nat rank two. Omitting small directions can also remove information about\nrare events; approximation quality is therefore not automatically\nprediction quality.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>With one million degrees of freedom and five non-zero entries per\nrow, the double-precision values alone occupy 40 MB (decimal); indices\nand row pointers are also required. Dense storage of the values alone\nwould require 8 TB. The memory savings do not imply any fixed iteration\ncount.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/\">Boyd/Vandenberghe:\nConvex Optimization (2004), Ch. 2–5, 9–11<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.sparse.linalg.cg.html\">SciPy:\ncg<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.sparse.linalg.lsqr.html\">SciPy:\nlsqr<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M9-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M9-L7 – Optimisation diagnostics and comparison of methods<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor: Numerical methods and optimisation for learning algorithms (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/compulsory elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L2<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L2<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Diagnose optimisation\ntrajectories and plan fair comparisons of methods.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Convergence is not the same as generalisation<\/h2>\n<p>A learning procedure can stop for several reasons: the solver reaches\na stationary optimum, a step limit, a numerical error or a validation\ncriterion. These cases must be logged separately. For a smooth\nunconstrained problem, suitable indicators include the gradient norm,\nabsolute or relative function change and step norm. A feasible\noptimisation point on the boundary may have a large gradient; projected\nor KKT residuals are then needed. A small step size may also arise from\nan excessively small learning rate.<\/p>\n<p>A relative change criterion can be defined as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|f_{k+1}-f_k|/\\max(1,|f_k|)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTogether with an absolute gradient criterion and a maximum number of\niterations, it prevents some erroneous success reports. The numerical\nscale and units of the parameters must match the tolerances. Checking\nfor finite values detects overflows, NaNs and invalid function\ndomains.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Diagnostics for two coupled directions<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001660_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Berechnete Kosten für drei Lernraten; bei 0,11 divergiert die Folge.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Berechnete Kosten für drei Lernraten; bei\n0,11 divergiert die Folge.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Cost trajectories for optimisation diagnostics.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(u,v)=\\frac12(u^2+20v^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nstarting at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na gradient step is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mi>η<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(u^+,v^+)=((1-\\eta)u,(1-20\\eta)v)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe second coordinate vanishes after one step, while the first shrinks\nslowly by a factor of 0.95. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.09<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=0.09<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe second factor is minus 0.8; the costs initially decrease faster or\nslower depending on the direction. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.11<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=0.11<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe magnitude of the second factor is 1.2: the costs eventually\nincrease. The diagram shows the actual losses on a logarithmic axis. A\nsingle first step or the step norm is insufficient for diagnosis.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reproducible comparison protocols<\/h2>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>H <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.diag([<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">20.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> eta <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [<span class=\"fl\">.05<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.09<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.11<\/span>]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    w <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.ones(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    history <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> []<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> k <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">41<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        history.append(<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> w <span class=\"op\">@<\/span> H <span class=\"op\">@<\/span> w)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        w <span class=\"op\">-=<\/span> eta <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> (H <span class=\"op\">@<\/span> w)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isfinite(history).<span class=\"bu\">all<\/span>()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(eta, history[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>For a fair empirical comparison, fix the data split, objective and\nregularisation, initialisation distribution, hardware, numerical\nprecision and stopping rules. Record runtime, the number of function and\ngradient evaluations, data points, peak memory and the achieved loss.\nRandomised methods require repetitions with predetermined seeds and\nmeasures of dispersion. Assess model quality using separate validation\ndata, and use a held-out test set only after selection. Early stopping\nserves a generalisation objective and is not proof of numerical\nconvergence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>One method uses 100 steps with 1,000 examples per step; another uses\n400 steps with 100 examples. These correspond to 100,000 and 40,000\nevaluated examples respectively. Which is faster? Without runtime and\ncost per example, this cannot be determined. Which generalises better?\nThat too cannot be determined without comparison on separate data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.cornell.edu/courses/cs3780/2026sp/lectures/lecturenote07.html\">Cornell\nCS 4/5780: Gradient Descent (and Beyond)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://stat243.berkeley.edu/fall-2024/units/unit11-optim.html\">UC\nBerkeley STAT 243: Optimization (2024)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.helsinki.fi/u/ahonkela/teaching/compstats1/book/automatic-differentiation-and-optimisation.html\">A.\nHonkela, Computational Statistics I: Automatic differentiation and\noptimisation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M9-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M9-L8 – Linear and combinatorial optimisation for data problems<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M9 – Bachelor: Numerical methods and optimisation for learning algorithms (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M9-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model linear programmes and\ninteger decisions and assess relaxation bounds.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Resource allocation in a data platform<\/h2>\n<p>In linear programming (LP), the objective function and constraints\nare linear. One application is selecting analysis jobs under limited\nhardware resources. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x,y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare divisible computing allocations, the utility\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3x+4y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis to be maximised. Memory and time requirements give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2x+y\\le5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x+2y\\le5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the simplified teaching model, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x,y\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe feasible set is a polyhedron. For a non-empty bounded polyhedron, a\nlinear objective has at least one optimal vertex. Infeasible, unbounded\nor multiply optimal problems require separate treatment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From continuous to discrete decisions<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001626_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Polygon mit zulässigen Gitterpunkten, LP-Optimum (5/3; 5/3), ILP-Optimum (1; 2) und unzulässiger Rundung (2; 2).\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Polygon mit zulässigen Gitterpunkten,\nLP-Optimum (5/3; 5/3), ILP-Optimum (1; 2) und unzulässiger Rundung (2;\n2).<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Linear relaxation and integer solution.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The vertices are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2.5,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(5/3,5/3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,2.5)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTheir utilities are 0, 7.5,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>35<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>11.667<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">35/3\\approx11.667<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand 10. If only whole jobs are allowed, we instead obtain the integer\nlinear programme (ILP) with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℤ<\/mi><mrow><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x,y\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe sole feasible lattice point with maximum value here is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith eleven. Rounding the LP solution to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nviolates both constraints. The diagram distinguishes the continuous\nfeasible region and the feasible discrete points within it. For the\nmaximisation problem, the LP value is an upper bound on the ILP value,\nnot necessarily attainable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solvers and combinatorial problems<\/h2>\n<p>Branch-and-bound examines subproblems by branching on integer\nvariables and using relaxed bounds to exclude unsuitable subregions.\nCuts can add further valid inequalities. In a knapsack problem, for\ninstance,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_i\\in\\{0,1\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndetermines whether a dataset is selected;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_i v_i z_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis maximised subject to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_i c_i z_i\\le B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAssignment problems use binary variables to pair two sets, while flow\nproblems use capacity and balance constraints. Not every combinatorial\nproblem is equally difficult; polynomial-time algorithms exist for some\nspecial structures, but general integer optimisation can require\nexponential effort.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.optimize <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> linprog, milp, LinearConstraint<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>A <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([[<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>], [<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>b <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">5.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">5.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>c <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">3.<\/span>, <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">4.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>lp <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> linprog(c, A_ub<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>A, b_ub<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>b, method<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;highs&#39;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>ilp <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> milp(c, integrality<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>           constraints<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>LinearConstraint(A, <span class=\"op\">-<\/span>np.inf, b))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> lp.success <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> ilp.success<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>lp.fun, <span class=\"dv\">35<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(ilp.x, [<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>]) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.isclose(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>ilp.fun, <span class=\"dv\">11<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The negative objective coefficients transform maximisation into\nminimisation. With time limits, a solver may return only an intermediate\nfeasible solution. The status, bound and optimality gap are then more\nimportant than merely displaying a result.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Which allocations are still possible for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\nThe first constraint gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\le1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the second\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\le1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence the maximum is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith value ten. The alternative point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis feasible and better. The absolute difference between the LP bound and\nthe ILP solution is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>35<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">35/3-11=2/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/\">Boyd/Vandenberghe:\nConvex Optimization (2004), Ch. 2–5, 9–11<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.optimize.linprog.html\">SciPy:\nlinprog<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.optimize.milp.html\">SciPy:\nmilp<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
